  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9=x(-2)y(-2)+x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1-2t$ and $q(t)=2-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V,$ then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8p(t),q(t)\u27e9 & =p(-2)q(-2)+p(0)q(0)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(1+4)(2-4k)+(1)(2)+(1-4)(2-4k) \\\\  & =(10-20k)+2+(-6+12k) \\\\  & =6-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8p(t),q(t)\u27e9=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8p(t),q(t)\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 6-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
